---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base
  title="The Hornby Claim"
  eyebrow="The pedigree, tested"
  dek="The family tree runs this line back to a Yorkshire castle and an earldom. This page sets out exactly where that descent stops being evidence and starts being hope — and it stops in a place the tree itself admits to.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Above <strong>Major George Pitt D'Arcy of the 39th Foot</strong> the family tree
    keeps climbing: to a Major-General of Royal Engineers, to a man said to be born in St James's
    Palace, to the <strong>Earls of Holderness</strong>, and finally to <strong>Hornby Castle</strong>
    in the North Riding of Yorkshire in the 1560s. Fifteen generations in all.</p>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">Six of those fifteen are documented to the day. The
    archive will defend them. The rest are a different kind of claim, and the honest thing is to say
    so on its own page rather than bury it in a footnote.</p>

    <blockquote>
      A pedigree that reaches a peerage is the most suspect kind of pedigree there is. Not because
      such descents never happen — they do — but because they are the descents people most want, and
      wanting is how a guess becomes a fact in three generations of retelling.
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The break</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Major-General Robert D'Arcy, and the sentence the tree wrote about him</h2>

    <p><strong>Robert D'Arcy (1751–1827)</strong> is real, and he is impressive. He was
    <em>Major-General and Commander of the Royal Engineers at Chatham</em>, a Companion of the Bath,
    known in the family as <em>The General</em>. He married Jean Ward at Portsea in 1779 and had ten
    children — among them <strong>Joseph</strong>, who went to Persia, and <strong>George Pitt</strong>,
    who went to New South Wales. All of that is solid.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1rem">His burial is now confirmed from the register itself. The
    <strong>Kent Burials</strong> transcript for <strong>St Mary, Chatham</strong> records
    <em>Robert D'Arcy, of Chatham, buried 19 May 1827, aged 76</em>.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>And that is where "1751" comes from.</strong> Not from a baptism — from an age stated
      on a burial register by whoever reported the death. It puts his birth between about May 1750
      and May 1751 and it names <em>no father</em>. Every version of this pedigree repeats 1751 as
      though it were a birth record. It is an arithmetic subtraction from a mourner's estimate.
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">What is not solid is where <em>he</em> came from. The family tree
    carries this note against his name:</p>

    <blockquote>
      A lineal descendant of the great and ancient baronial house of D'Arcy, in which vested the
      Earldom of Holderness. <strong>… this Robert thought to be the illegitim[ate]…</strong>
    </blockquote>

    <p><strong>"Thought to be."</strong> The tree is not asserting a documented descent here. It is
    recording a supposition — that Robert was an illegitimate son of a Darcy Earl of Holderness — and
    the supposition has since been drawn as a solid line on a chart, which is how suppositions get
    promoted.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>And the tree contradicts itself.</strong> The note points at the <em>4th</em> Earl
      (Robert Darcy, 1718–1778). But the tree's actual structure hangs Robert not off the 4th Earl at
      all — it inserts a <em>Thomas (Francis) D'Arcy</em>, said to be born at St James's Palace, and
      descends him from the <em>3rd</em> Earl. Two different claims about the same join, in the same
      file. At most one of them can be right, and neither carries a document.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Why the peerage line matters to the arithmetic</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The earldom of Holderness ended in 1778, in a daughter</h2>
    <p>The Darcy Earls of Holderness are among the best-recorded families in England. Conyers Darcy,
    1st Earl (1598–1689); Conyers, 2nd Earl (1622–1692); Robert, 3rd Earl (1681–1721); Robert, 4th
    Earl (1718–1778). At the 4th Earl's death the earldom became <strong>extinct</strong>, because he
    left no legitimate son — his heir was his daughter Amelia, who carried the barony of Conyers into
    another family by marriage.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1rem">The 4th Earl married Mary Doublet in 1743 and had three children:
    <strong>George, Lord Darcy and Conyers, who died in 1747 aged two; Thomas, Lord Darcy and Conyers,
    born and died in 1750; and Lady Amelia</strong>, the only one to reach adulthood. Both sons died
    in infancy.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <strong>A coincidence to notice and then put down.</strong> The Earl's second son was born and
      died in <strong>1750</strong>; our Robert D'Arcy was born about <strong>1750 or 1751</strong>.
      That is the kind of near-miss out of which family traditions are built, and it is not evidence
      of anything. It is recorded because someone else will notice it, and should find it already
      weighed.
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">The extinction is the whole reason an <em>illegitimate</em> son has to
    be invoked at all. A legitimate male descent from the 4th Earl is not merely unproven; it is
    excluded by the peerage record. So the claim reduces to: <em>somewhere in the 1750s an earl
    fathered a boy outside marriage, that boy became a Major-General of Engineers, and nobody wrote it
    down.</em> Possible. Common enough, even. But it is precisely the kind of claim that leaves no
    paper — which is what makes it so attractive and so useless.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What this archive is prepared to say</h2>
    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip doc">documented</span> Robert D'Arcy existed and was a General</h3>
        <p>Major-General Robert D'Arcy C.B., Royal Engineers, Chatham. Married Jean Ward, Portsea,
        21 June 1779; died Chatham 13 May 1827. Father of Joseph of Persia and George Pitt of the
        39th Foot. This man is the top of the archive's defensible line.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip dis">disputed</span> That he was a Darcy of Holderness</h3>
        <p>Asserted, twice, in two incompatible ways, with no document behind either. Until a baptism,
        a will, a settlement or an admission is produced, this archive treats the Yorkshire descent as
        <em>family tradition</em> and nothing more.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip lore">unproven</span> Thomas (Francis) D'Arcy of St James's Palace</h3>
        <p>The inserted generation. Born, it is said, in a royal palace in 1710 or 1681 — the tree
        gives both — and dead in 1776. A person with two birth years sixty-nine years apart is not yet
        a person; he is a placeholder.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip inf">inferred</span> The name itself is a hint, not a proof</h3>
        <p>The apostrophe form <em>D'Arcy</em> leans Norman rather than Gaelic. But clerks added and
        dropped it freely for four hundred years, and Galway's D'Arcys were Ó Dorchaidhe who spelled
        themselves Norman. <a href={u("/name")}>The name →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Searched, and found empty</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">There is no baptism</h2>
    <p>FindMyPast has been swept for the <strong>baptism of a Robert D'Arcy or Darcy born
    c. 1741–1761</strong>, name variants on, across every English record set. The sweep returns
    burials, marriages, wills and Irish abstracts — <strong>and not one English baptism in the
    window</strong>, in Yorkshire or anywhere else.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">A null is a result. It does not disprove the Yorkshire descent; it
    does mean the descent <em>cannot presently be reached from below</em>, and that the argument has
    to be made, if it can be made at all, from military and estate papers instead of parish
    registers. That reorders the queue.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">Two further negatives from this session, both worth recording:</p>
    <ul class="dek">
      <li>The <em>Dictionary of National Biography</em> entry headed "<strong>D'Arcy, Robert</strong>"
      is <strong>the 4th Earl of Holderness himself</strong> — ambassador to Venice, Secretary of
      State for the Southern Department 1751–57, Governor of the Prince of Wales. It is not our
      Major-General, who has no DNB entry.</li>
      <li>A published compilation of Royal Engineers officer biographies drawn from
      <strong>Connolly's <em>Roll of Officers of the Corps of Royal Engineers, 1660–1898</em></strong>
      contains <strong>no Robert D'Arcy or Darcy</strong> — but that compilation covers roughly 1800
      onward, so a man commissioned in the 1770s falls outside it. <strong>Connolly's Roll itself has
      not yet been searched</strong>, and it is now the single most promising unread source in this
      archive. A copy is at HathiTrust.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.9rem">One thing the same sweep did turn up: <em>England,
    Boyd's Marriage Indexes</em>, London, 1779 — "D'Arcy, Robert Hon Amelia Baroness Conyers". That
    is the 4th Earl's daughter <strong>Amelia, Baroness Conyers</strong>, marrying in the very year
    Robert D'Arcy married Jean Ward at Portsea. It is the documentary trace of the legitimate line
    passing to a daughter — which is precisely why an illegitimate son has to be invoked here at
    all.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What would actually settle it</h2>
    <ol class="dek">
      <li><strong>Robert D'Arcy's baptism, c. 1750–51.</strong> <span class="chip lore">searched, not found</span>
      A parish register naming a father — or naming a mother and no father, which would be evidence
      of the illegitimacy the tradition claims. Swept on FindMyPast across all English record sets
      with no result, so any further search needs to go to unindexed originals: the Bishop's
      Transcripts for the North Riding, and the garrison and chapel registers of the towns where
      artillery families lived.</li>
      <li><strong>His Royal Engineers service record.</strong> Commissions and cadet papers of the
      1760s–70s at the National Archives frequently name a parent or a patron. A patron of the right
      surname would be the strongest indirect evidence available.</li>
      <li><strong>The 4th Earl's will and estate papers.</strong> Provision for a natural son is the
      classic form such an acknowledgement takes.</li>
      <li><strong>A Y-DNA test against a documented Darcy of Holderness male line</strong> — if one
      survives, which given the extinction of the earldom is itself an open question.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1rem">Until then the line stands at 1751 with a General, and
    that is a perfectly good place for a family line to stand.
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>The research queue →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
